<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dada</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1916">1916</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Dadaism</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dada was an avant-garde art movement founded in 1916 at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cabaret-voltaire">Cabaret Voltaire</a> in Zurich by artists who had taken refuge in neutral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. Rejecting reason and bourgeois art, which they blamed for the war, its members, among them Hugo Ball, Tristan Tzara and Hans Arp, used nonsense, chance and collage. It spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> and prepared the way for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/surrealism">Surrealism</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dada.jpg" alt="Dada" width="320" height="267" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grand_opening_of_the_first_Dada_exhibition,_Berlin,_5_June_1920.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Dada developed in the context of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, its aftermath and the Futurist movement. Its principles were first collected in Hugo Ball's Dada Manifesto of 1916, and Ball is seen as the founder. Most art historians trace its origin to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cabaret-voltaire">Cabaret Voltaire</a> in Zürich, co-founded by the poet and cabaret singer Emmy Hennings and Ball, and named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a>, whose novel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/candide">Candide</a> mocked the dogmas of his day. Opening night was attended by Ball, Hennings, Tristan Tzara, Jean Arp and Marcel Janco. There is no single agreed origin for the name: one story says that Richard Huelsenbeck pushed a paper knife into a dictionary and landed on the French word dada, meaning hobby horse.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Participants framed their activity as a protest against war, nationalism and cultural conformity, and used nonsense, chance and ridicule to negate prevailing aesthetic values. Dadaists worked in sound poetry, collage and photomontage, found objects and assemblage. They invented the chance collage, made by dropping torn scraps of paper on a larger sheet and pasting them where they landed. Tzara's manifesto of 1920 proposed writing poems from words cut out of a newspaper and drawn at random. Ball invented sound poetry. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, Marcel Duchamp's readymades became emblematic of its anti-art stance.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The movement spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> and other centres, each with its own emphasis: performance and poetry in Zürich, politically charged photomontage in Berlin, and object-based experiments in New York. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>, Max Ernst, Johannes Baargeld and Arp held an exhibition in 1920 in a pub, which the police closed on grounds of obscenity until the charges were dropped. By 1924 in Paris Dada was melding into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/surrealism">Surrealism</a>. It influenced pop art, Fluxus, the Situationist International and avant-garde composers such as John Cage, and some theorists argue that it was the beginning of postmodern art.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dada” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Zurich saw the birth of <b>Dada</b> at the Cabaret Voltaire in 1916, and Swiss authors such as Gottfried Keller, Max Frisch and Friedrich Dürrenmatt, the psychiatrist Carl Gustav Jung and the architect Le Corbusier gained wide reputations.</blockquote><a href="/empire/swiss-confederation">Swiss Confederation · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The First World War scattered the writers and artists who had been based in Paris, and many became involved with <b>Dada</b>, believing that excessive rational thought and bourgeois values had brought the conflict upon the world.</blockquote><a href="/movement/surrealism">Surrealism · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>It is reported to have been written around 1979 and first appears on a demo tape known as the &quot;<b>Dada</b> Demos&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/redemption-song">Redemption Song</a></li></ul></section>
